@@ -0,0 +1,186 @@
+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report — `symphonia-core` v0.6.0
+
+Tests were added with the hegel skill workflow (`hegeltest` 0.28.2 as a
+dev-dependency of the `symphonia-core` workspace member). All tests live in the
+existing unit-test modules of the files whose code they test: `src/io/bit.rs`,
+`src/io/buf_reader.rs`, `src/audio/conv.rs`, and `src/util.rs`. 19 hegel tests
+covering ~13 distinct properties.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+### Bit readers (`src/io/bit.rs`, 11 tests)
+
+The oracle throughout is the buffer's bit sequence computed byte-by-byte in
+test code: MSb-first per byte for `BitReaderLtr`, LSb-first for `BitReaderRtl`.
+
+1. **`hegel_ltr_read_bit_matches_byte_oracle` / `hegel_rtl_read_bit_matches_byte_oracle`**
+   — reading every bit with `read_bit` reproduces the oracle bit sequence, and
+   the read past the last bit is a clean `Err` (never a panic).
+   *Evidence:* the struct docs define the exact bit order for each reader.
+
+2. **`hegel_ltr_read_bits_matches_bit_oracle` / `hegel_rtl_read_bits_matches_bit_oracle`**
+   — chunked reads with a drawn width schedule (0..=64 bits, cycling, using
+   `read_bits_leq32` for widths ≤ 32 and `read_bits_leq64` above) equal the
+   value assembled from the oracle bit slice (MSb-first for Ltr, LSb-first for
+   Rtl). After every read, `FiniteBitStream::bits_left` equals total bits minus
+   bits consumed.
+   *Evidence:* method docs plus the existing hand-written `verify_*_read_bits_*`
+   unit tests, generalized.
+
+3. **`hegel_ignore_bits_equals_reading_bits`** — for any n ≤ total bits,
+   `ignore_bits(n)` leaves the reader positioned so the remaining bit sequence
+   is the oracle tail `bits[n..]`; ignoring more bits than the stream holds
+   errors. Both readers.
+   *Evidence:* `ignore_bits` doc ("Ignores the specified number of bits from
+   the stream or returns an error"), existing `verify_*_ignore_bits` tests.
+
+4. **`hegel_read_unary_zeros_matches_oracle` / `hegel_read_unary_ones_matches_oracle`**
+   — decoding unary codes until exhaustion yields exactly the run lengths of
+   0s (resp. 1s) delimited by the opposite bit in the oracle bit sequence, and
+   the read after the last complete code errors (trailing run with no
+   terminator). Both readers.
+
+5. **`hegel_read_unary_capped_matches_oracle`** — `read_unary_*_capped(limit)`
+   returns `min(leading run, limit)`; when capped it consumes exactly `limit`
+   bits, otherwise the run plus its terminator (verified by draining the tail
+   bit-by-bit against the oracle); when the uncapped run reaches end-of-stream
+   it errors. All four reader × polarity combinations.
+
+6. **`hegel_arbitrary_ops_never_panic`** — any drawn schedule of reader
+   operations (`read_bit/bool`, `read_bits_leq32/64` and signed variants,
+   `ignore_bits`, unary reads incl. capped, `realign`) on any buffer, including
+   far past end-of-stream, returns `Ok`/`Err` and never panics. This exercises
+   post-error reader states the unit tests never reach.
+
+7. **`hegel_codebook_roundtrip`** — the flagship generated-configuration test:
+   a random *complete* prefix code is built by repeated leaf splitting (up to
+   49 codewords, ≤ 14 bits), a random `bits_per_read` (1..=16) is drawn, a
+   random symbol sequence is encoded to bits in test code, and
+   `read_codebook` must return each symbol and its code length — for
+   `BitOrder::Verbatim` + `BitReaderLtr` and `BitOrder::Reverse` +
+   `BitReaderRtl` (the MP3 and Vorbis configurations respectively).
+   *Evidence:* `CodebookBuilder::make` docs; usage in symphonia-bundle-mp3
+   (Verbatim) and symphonia-codec-vorbis (Reverse).
+
+8. **`hegel_read_codebook_arbitrary_data_consumes_all_bytes`** — hegel-driven
+   version of the existing seeded `fuzz_bitstream*_read_codebook` tests (kept,
+   per the skill's supplement-don't-delete guidance): decoding arbitrary bytes
+   until error never panics and consumes the whole buffer.
+
+### Byte reader (`src/io/buf_reader.rs`, 1 test)
+
+9. **`hegel_buf_reader_matches_slice_oracle`** — model test: a drawn schedule
+   of `ReadBytes` operations (`read_u8/u16/be_u16/u24/be_u24/u32/be_u32/u64/
+   be_u64`, `ignore_bytes`, `read_buf`) against a slice-cursor model using
+   `from_le_bytes`/`from_be_bytes` as the value oracle. Checks returned values,
+   that underruns error *without moving the position*, that `read_buf` returns
+   `min(remaining, requested)`, and that `pos()` tracks the model exactly.
+
+### Sample-format conversions (`src/audio/conv.rs`, 3 tests)
+
+10. **`hegel_widening_roundtrip_is_identity`** — converting any sample to any
+    strictly wider (lossless) format and back is the identity, for every such
+    pair among {i8,u8,i16,u16,i24,u24,i32,u32,f32,f64} (39 pairs; float
+    intermediates only where the mantissa can hold the integer width).
+    *Evidence:* the `FromSample` scaling implementations are exact
+    powers-of-two shifts/divisions for these pairs; existing `verify_*` tests
+    pin MIN/MID/MAX only.
+
+11. **`hegel_signed_unsigned_same_width_roundtrip`** — the offset-binary
+    signed↔unsigned mapping at equal width (i8↔u8 … i32↔u32) is a bijection.
+
+12. **`hegel_float_conversion_is_path_independent`** — `f64::from_sample(x)`
+    (and `f32` where exact) equals conversion via any lossless wider integer
+    intermediate, e.g. `f64::from_sample(i32::from_sample(x))` for i8/i16/u8/
+    u16/i24/u24 sources. A wrong scaling constant on any path would break the
+    agreement.
+
+### Utilities (`src/util.rs`, 4 tests)
+
+13. **`hegel_sign_extend_matches_arithmetic_oracle`** — all four
+    `sign_extend_leq*` functions equal an arithmetic two's-complement oracle
+    (`masked - 2^width` when the sign bit is set) for widths 1..=N, with bits
+    above `width` drawn arbitrarily (they must be ignored).
+
+14. **`hegel_masks_match_bit_loop_oracle`** — every `mask_*` function equals a
+    mask built bit-by-bit from its documented predicate; `mask_range(upper,
+    lower)` masks exactly `[lower, upper)` for all `lower <= upper <= 8`.
+
+15. **`hegel_contains_ones_byte_matches_oracle`** — the SWAR
+    `contains_ones_byte_u16/u32/u64` tricks agree with
+    `to_le_bytes().contains(&0xff)`; because random values rarely contain an
+    all-ones byte, values with a 0xff byte forced in at a drawn position are
+    also constructed directly (per the skill's construct-degenerate-cases
+    rule).
+
+16. **`hegel_clamp_matches_std_clamp`** — every branchless `clamp_*` function
+    agrees with `std`'s `clamp`/`min` over the full input domain (floats drawn
+    with infinities, without NaN).
+
+## Test results
+
+- Full `cargo test -p symphonia-core --lib`: **82 passed, 0 failed** (63
+  pre-existing + 19 hegel).
+- Liveness verified in two inversion rounds: round A broke one assertion in
+  six different tests (including assertions *behind guards*: the chunked-read
+  loop body, the capped-unary limit branch, the BufReader success branch, the
+  codebook decode loop) — all six failed with minimal shrunk counterexamples
+  (e.g. `buf=[], widths=[0]`; `num_splits=1, seq=[0]`). Round B broke the
+  secondary branches (the `read_bits_leq64` w>32 path, the capped-unary
+  terminator and error branches, the BufReader underrun branch, the Rtl
+  codebook loop, the i24 draw path) — all fired. All inversions reverted.
+- Exploratory 10x run (`test_cases = 1000` on all 19 tests): all green;
+  reverted to the default 100 for the committed tests.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+None found. `symphonia-core`'s bit readers, byte reader, sample converters,
+and bit utilities held up against independent oracles at boundaries (empty
+buffers, 0- and 64-bit reads, cache-refill seams, i32::MIN/MAX, forced 0xff
+bytes, reads past end-of-stream, post-error reader states).
+
+One documentation nit (not pinned by a failing test since the code is
+correct): the doc comment on `util::clamp::clamp_i16` says it clamps to
+"[-32_767, 32_768]" — the bounds are transposed; the implementation clamps to
+[-32_768, 32_767], which is what the property verified.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **i32/u32 round-trip through f32** — deliberately excluded from the
+  widening round-trip property: f32 has a 24-bit significand, so the
+  conversion is genuinely lossy per the module docs ("may be a lossy
+  conversion"), not a bug. Only float intermediates wide enough for exactness
+  were asserted.
+- **`sign_extend_leq*` with `width = 0`** — excluded (width drawn from 1..=N).
+  With width 0 the `wrapping_shl(N)` shift wraps to a no-op and the value is
+  returned unextended; a "0-bit two's complement integer" is outside any
+  sensible reading of the documented domain.
+- **`mask_range` with `lower > upper`** — constrained to `lower <= upper`; the
+  doc comment's "(upper < bit <= lower)" is itself garbled, and the unit tests
+  only establish semantics for `lower <= upper`.
+- **`realign()` position oracle** — not asserted; realign is defined relative
+  to the reader's internal 64-bit cache refills, not the source byte
+  positions, so a buffer-based oracle would assert more than the API
+  promises. It is still exercised for no-panic in the ops test.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The "generate configurations, not just payloads" rule paid off directly:
+  drawing `bits_per_read` (1..=16) in the codebook round-trip exercises jump
+  chains, do-not-care padding, and single-block layouts that the crate's fixed
+  `bits_per_block = 4` tests never touch. `Entry16x32` was chosen after
+  reasoning that a u16 jump index can overflow for bits_per_read 9..=13 —
+  worth a catalogue note: when generating table-builder configs, size the
+  index type for the worst generated case or the test fails on builder
+  errors, not real bugs.
+- The existing seeded `rand` fuzz tests (`fuzz_bitstream*_read_codebook`) were
+  exactly the pattern the skill flags for evolution; they were kept and
+  supplemented with a hegel-driven equivalent as advised.
+- Guarded-branch liveness checking (inverting asserts inside guards, two
+  rounds) caught nothing wrong here but was cheap and confirmed the capped
+  unary error branch fires via the empty-buffer + limit>0 case — a
+  non-obvious vacuity risk.
+- Environment note: `cargo add --dev hegeltest` blocked on the shared
+  package-cache lock (parallel agents); pinning `hegeltest = "0.28.2"` by
+  editing `Cargo.toml` (version already in the local registry) avoided the
+  wait.
